-- title:
--   Unit cubes over points of a $\sqrt{d}$-shrunk set stay in the half-shrunk set
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$. For a set $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ and a radius $r$, write
--   $$S_r \;=\; \{\, y \in S \;:\; \overline{B}(y, r) \subseteq S \,\}$$
--   for the *shrinking* of $S$ by $r$ — the points of $S$ that stay in $S$ after a closed ball of radius $r$ is placed around them — and write $S[x] = x + S$ for the translate of $S$ by $x$.
--
--   Let $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and suppose
--   $$y \in \big(S_{\sqrt{d}}\big)[x] = x + S_{\sqrt{d}}.$$
--   Then the open unit cube centred at $y$ satisfies
--   $$A_1(y) \;\subseteq\; \big(S_{\sqrt{d}/2}\big)[x] = x + S_{\sqrt{d}/2}.$$
--
--   In words: if the centre of a unit cube survives shrinking by $\sqrt{d}$, then the entire cube survives shrinking by $\sqrt{d}/2$. The gap between the two radii is exactly the circumradius $\tfrac12\sqrt{d}$ of the unit cube, so the statement is the quantitative form of "a whole tile is only slightly worse placed than its centre". It is what allows a combinatorial argument carried out on lattice centres to be upgraded to a statement about the tiles those centres index, without leaving the region under consideration.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis and conclusion are both stated for a translate of $S$ by the same point $x$, so the lemma is genuinely about the shrinking operation and not about the translation. The case $h = 1$ of the scaled version.
